# /etc/apivo/<env>/blnk.env — 0600 root:root
#
# The ledger's own configuration, read by the Docker daemon as the `blnk` and
# `blnk-worker` services' `env_file` (deploy/hetzner/compose/docker-compose.cashback.yml).
# It is never sourced by the reconciler and never appears in
# `docker compose config` output, so the ledger's database password does not
# travel through a shell that also writes logs — the same rule api.env exists
# for, applied to the same kind of value.
#
# Blnk accepts either a /blnk.json file or environment variables prefixed
# `BLNK_`. The environment form is used here because exactly one key in the
# set is a credential, and a credential belongs in a 0600 file the daemon
# reads rather than in a JSON document this repository would have to template.
#
# BLNK_REDIS_DNS is deliberately ABSENT: it is a container name the compose
# file chose, not an operator's decision, so it is set in the compose file
# alongside the api's BLNK_URL. Adding it here would create a second place to
# change one address.
#
# Written as a template by provision.sh and then never touched again — like
# api.env, and for the same reason: a script that could fill in the DSN would
# be a script that had to be given it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BLNK_DATA_SOURCE_DNS — the ledger's Postgres connection string.
#
# ADR-0002 puts Blnk in the SAME Postgres instance as the api, in its own
# `blnk` schema with its own restricted role. One database, one backup, one
# point-in-time recovery, and the C-1 zero-sum check stays a plain SQL query
# over real rows instead of a distributed reconciliation. That co-location is
# subject to spike S1; if S1 fails, the recorded fallback is a Blnk-owned
# Postgres, which changes this line and adds a service to the compose overlay
# — it does not change the application.
#
# The role is NOT the api's role. `blnk` owns the `blnk` schema and must have
# no rights in `public`; that separation is what makes "Blnk's migrations
# never touch public" an enforceable property rather than a hope.
#
#   qa       the Postgres container on this host, which serves TLS with a
#            self-signed certificate provision.sh generates:
#
#            postgres://blnk:<password>@postgres:5432/apivo?sslmode=require&search_path=blnk
#
#   staging  the same Supabase EU project the api uses, with a `blnk` role
#   prod     created in it:
#
#            postgres://blnk:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/postgres?sslmode=verify-full&search_path=blnk
#
# `sslmode` matters here for the same reason it matters in api.env: every
# example connection string in this repository ends in sslmode=disable, so the
# value most likely to be pasted into a deployment is the one that must never
# be there. Unlike the api, Blnk does not refuse to start on a cleartext URL —
# nothing enforces this but the person editing the file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BLNK_DATA_SOURCE_DNS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything else Blnk exposes has a documented default and is left unset on
# purpose. The ones worth knowing exist, should an incident ask for them:
# transaction batching and locking, queue sharding and retries, reconciliation
# strategy, webhook delivery, and backups. All are `BLNK_`-prefixed and all
# are documented at https://docs.blnkfinance.com/advanced/configuration.
#
# Blnk's own webhook and Typesense search features are NOT used: cashback
# reads its evidence from polled network transactions (ADR-0003) and searches
# nothing through the ledger. Leaving them unconfigured is the intended state,
# not an oversight to be tidied up later.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
